Mathematically speaking, if the success rate for wand
and lamp wishes had been identical, that would be
rather surprising -- not impossible, but a rare event.

The technical explanation is, this:  since the number of
possible even distributions is much lower than the total
number of possible distributions (i.e., most of the possible
distributions are not entirely even), and since a random
distribution is not more likely to elect any particular
possible distribution over any other, uneven distribution
is a significantly more likely outcome than an even
distribution.  If even distributions were favored over
uneven ones, the distribution wouldn't be random.

You can confirm this by rolling dice a few times in
a row.  You won't (usually) get a carefully-designed
even distribution.  You'll get... whatever you get.


